config - print details of your machine and C compiler  configura-tion  config  Config determines and prints out several propertiesof the C compiler it is compiled with and the machine it  is  runon.  Among the properties it gives are    - the number of bits in a char, and whether chars are  signed    or not;    - the maximum short, int, and long;    - alignment values for char, short, int and long;    - number of bits for char and int pointers, with a warning if    they are longer than int;and then for float and double:    - the base used;    - number of significant digits;    - certain minumum and maximum values;    - whether arithmetic rounds or chops;    - whether a hidden bit is used or not;    - etc.Finally the total amount of memory that can be allocated by  mal-loc(3)	is  printed.  The output is printed as a series of C com-ments, so that the program can be used to  produce  header  filesfor C programs without too much upheaval.  The program won't workif overflow causes a trap, and the trap can't be caught  by  sig-nal(2).